 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**I’d skip a hard outdoor ride this evening.** At 12:00 p.m. in Kuching on **Saturday, October 17**, the reported Malaysian Air Pollutant Index (API) was **66 (“moderate”)**, but IQAir’s separate **US AQI was 133 (“unhealthy for sensitive groups”)**. Those are different scales, so the numbers shouldn’t be compared directly. Neither noon reading can tell us what the air will be like when you set off. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/stations/kuching-sarawak/))\n\nIf you’re generally healthy and still want to cycle, **check the latest Kuching API just before leaving**. If it remains below 100, keep the ride short and easy; if it reaches **101 or higher**, choose an indoor ride instead. Malaysia’s health ministry advises avoiding outdoor physical activity when API exceeds 100. If you have asthma or a heart or lung condition, I’d choose indoors given the haze. Stop if you develop coughing, shortness of breath or chest discomfort. ([moh.gov.my](https://www.moh.gov.my/teras/info-kesihatan/jerebu/artikel-dan-panduan-pengguna-berkaitan-jerebu/kekal-aktif-walaupun-keadaan-berjerebu?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe forecast also calls for **thunderstorms this afternoon**, so check the sky and radar before riding. ",
